1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,084
Welcome to Episode Ten, subtopic seven point six: SMT Divergence.

2
00:00:07,504 --> 00:00:12,112
Every concept so far looked at one chart. SMT compares two.

3
00:00:12,532 --> 00:00:21,436
We will define it, set a clear convention, learn how to choose a valid pair, and see how it fits with liquidity, structure and PD arrays.

4
00:00:23,256 --> 00:00:30,984
SMT Divergence is a relative comparison of swing highs and lows between related instruments, over the same period.

5
00:00:31,404 --> 00:00:36,588
In ICT terminology, SMT stands for Smart Money Technique.

6
00:00:37,008 --> 00:00:43,560
Related markets usually move together. When they reach a swing point together, we expect similar behaviour.

7
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:52,020
When one makes a new extreme and the other does not, that disagreement is information about relative strength and weakness.

8
00:00:52,440 --> 00:00:58,608
This is not RSI or MACD divergence. No indicator is involved.

9
00:00:59,028 --> 00:01:06,756
And it is an analytical comparison. It does not prove institutional activity, and it does not predict a reversal on its own.

10
00:01:08,576 --> 00:01:17,288
SMT is only as good as the pair. First, do they share a similar underlying exposure, and a real economic relationship?

11
00:01:17,708 --> 00:01:22,772
Second, do they have a history of moving together? Check it; do not assume it.

12
00:01:23,192 --> 00:01:28,760
Third, do they trade at the same time? Different session hours create false divergences.

13
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:34,004
Fourth, are the data, contract specifications and currencies comparable?

14
00:01:34,424 --> 00:01:38,120
Fifth, what drivers do they share, and where do they differ?

15
00:01:38,540 --> 00:01:46,148
And remember, correlation is not constant. Never compare unrelated instruments just because their charts look alike.

16
00:01:47,968 --> 00:01:56,296
Bullish SMT. Two related instruments, on the same timeframe and the same time axis. Both have made a first swing low.

17
00:01:56,716 --> 00:02:04,612
Instrument A falls again and trades below its first low. It makes a lower low, sweeping the sell-side liquidity beneath.

18
00:02:05,032 --> 00:02:11,368
At the same time, Instrument B fails to make a corresponding lower low. It holds a higher low.

19
00:02:11,788 --> 00:02:20,980
That disagreement is the bullish SMT Divergence. B is showing relative strength, a warning that the bearish move may be weakening.

20
00:02:21,400 --> 00:02:29,080
Further confirmation is still needed. Here, Instrument A later shifts structure, closing above its last lower high.

21
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:41,212
Definitions vary on which instrument leads. The CMI convention: A is the instrument that makes the new extreme, B fails to confirm, and we look for confirmation on A.

22
00:02:43,032 --> 00:02:48,360
Bearish SMT is the opposite. Both instruments make a first swing high.

23
00:02:48,780 --> 00:02:56,268
Instrument A rallies above its first high, a higher high. Instrument B fails to follow, and prints a lower high.

24
00:02:56,688 --> 00:03:04,656
That is bearish SMT Divergence: relative weakness, a warning that the bullish move may be losing strength.

25
00:03:05,076 --> 00:03:10,284
Again, confirmation is needed. Here Instrument A later shifts structure lower.

26
00:03:10,704 --> 00:03:15,240
Without that confirmation, the divergence is only an observation.

27
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:25,124
SMT connects naturally with Level Three liquidity. Both instruments have sell-side liquidity under a previous low.

28
00:03:25,544 --> 00:03:27,248
Instrument A sweeps it.

29
00:03:27,668 --> 00:03:32,228
Instrument B does not. Its sell-side liquidity remains intact.

30
00:03:32,648 --> 00:03:45,296
The same applies with buy-side liquidity in bearish SMT. But evaluate it within the broader market. Not every mismatch between two highs or lows is meaningful SMT.

31
00:03:47,116 --> 00:03:55,060
Now link SMT with Level Two structure. On Instrument A, the bearish leg prints a lower high and a lower low.

32
00:03:55,480 --> 00:04:01,384
The SMT divergence appears at that lower low, because Instrument B holds its low.

33
00:04:01,804 --> 00:04:06,532
Structure then confirms with a market structure shift above the lower high.

34
00:04:06,952 --> 00:04:09,496
A new higher high would extend the shift.

35
00:04:09,916 --> 00:04:15,916
SMT adds context around a possible shift. It does not replace structure analysis.

36
00:04:17,736 --> 00:04:25,920
SMT is often studied at PD arrays. Here, Instrument A trades into a higher-timeframe bullish order block.

37
00:04:26,340 --> 00:04:28,380
The order block sits in discount.

38
00:04:28,800 --> 00:04:35,184
At the same time, the SMT divergence forms: A makes a lower low, B does not.

39
00:04:35,604 --> 00:04:39,036
Displacement follows and leaves a fair value gap.

40
00:04:39,456 --> 00:04:46,680
SMT at a PD array is an interesting analytical confluence. It does not guarantee a reaction.

41
00:04:48,500 --> 00:05:04,100
How does SMT differ from RSI or MACD divergence? SMT compares one instrument’s price with a related instrument’s price. Oscillator divergence compares price with an indicator calculated from that same price.

42
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:13,712
SMT uses relative highs and lows across instruments. Oscillator divergence uses price extremes versus indicator extremes.

43
00:05:14,132 --> 00:05:22,196
SMT depends on the relationship between instruments. Oscillator divergence depends on the indicator formula and settings.

44
00:05:22,616 --> 00:05:28,640
SMT highlights relative strength or weakness. Oscillator divergence highlights momentum.

45
00:05:29,060 --> 00:05:32,540
They are separate techniques, with separate failure modes.

46
00:05:34,360 --> 00:05:38,152
Common mistakes. Comparing unrelated instruments.

47
00:05:38,572 --> 00:05:41,668
Using different or misaligned timeframes.

48
00:05:42,088 --> 00:05:46,672
Ignoring session differences, and comparing data that is not synchronised.

49
00:05:47,092 --> 00:05:49,468
Assuming correlation is permanent.

50
00:05:49,888 --> 00:05:53,128
Treating every mismatch as SMT.

51
00:05:53,548 --> 00:05:57,388
Treating SMT as an automatic reversal signal.

52
00:05:57,808 --> 00:06:03,304
Confusing SMT with RSI or MACD divergence.

53
00:06:03,724 --> 00:06:06,196
And ignoring the broader market structure.

54
00:06:08,016 --> 00:06:16,848
To summarise. SMT compares the swings of related instruments, on the same timeframe, the same time axis, and the same swing.

55
00:06:17,268 --> 00:06:24,732
Bullish: A makes a lower low while B holds a higher low. Bearish: A makes a higher high while B makes a lower high.

56
00:06:25,152 --> 00:06:33,312
Look for confirmation on A with structure. And remember: it is not oscillator divergence, and it does not guarantee a reversal.

57
